{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789392262,"nonce":"k7U+gWgSPYGHtBGaLc3V6SyYR7U=","title":"A floor correction on a value already known non-negative","message":"Python's `%` by a positive constant lowers to a truncating `rem` and a\ncorrection taken when the remainder is negative (b2a2e27). When the\ndividend is itself the result of such a correction, or a non-negative\nconstant, or a loop phi of such values, the remainder is never negative\nand the correction decides nothing. In bench_branchy.py the first of\nthe two corrections per iteration (`k = acc % 7`, with `acc` the\nprevious iteration's `% 1000003`) is one such.\n\n`sign_fold` finds the values a function can show non-negative (the\ngreatest set closed under: non-negative constants, floor corrections,\nrem/div/and/add/mul of non-negatives, right shifts, selects between\nnon-negatives, phis of non-negatives) and folds `lt v, 0` to false and\n`ge v, 0` to true on them, then takes the arm of any select whose\ncondition is now constant. Sums and products count as non-negative\nshort of the integer wrap, which the program was not written for\neither.\n\nbranchy 95 to 92 ms; the second correction stays because `acc - 5` may\nbe negative and Python says so. The rest of branchy's gap to ZynML (72)\nis that correction on the loop-carried chain.","files":null}]}